export const DATA_81: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-120", num: 120, title: "Đề 120", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Mù Cang Chải: A Jewel of Vietnam's Northern Highlands", paragraphs: ["Nestled in the Yen Bai province of northwestern Vietnam, Mù Cang Chải is a district that has captured the imagination of travelers from across Vietnam and around the world. Located at an average altitude of approximately one thousand metres above sea level and bordered by the Hoang Lien Son mountain range, the area is best known for its breathtaking terraced rice fields, which cascade down the slopes of the valleys in sweeping, curved formations. These terraces, many of which were carved by hand over generations by the H'Mong ethnic minority communities who have inhabited the region for centuries, now extend across more than two thousand hectares and are recognized as a National Scenic Heritage Site by the Vietnamese government. For visitors who make the journey to this remote corner of the country, the landscape offers an experience unlike almost anything else in Southeast Asia.", "The best time to visit Mù Cang Chải is widely agreed to be during the harvest season, which typically falls between late September and early October. During this period, the terraced fields transform from vivid green into a rich patchwork of gold and amber as the ripening rice catches the light of the autumn sun. Photographers and nature lovers travel from great distances to capture this annual spectacle, and the winding mountain road that leads into the district becomes noticeably busier during these weeks. Outside the harvest season, the district also attracts visitors during the planting season in May and June, when the flooded terraces reflect the sky in still, mirror-like sheets of water that create an equally striking visual effect. Even during the quieter months, the dramatic mountain scenery and the cultural richness of the local communities ensure that Mù Cang Chải rewards those who seek it out.", "The H'Mong people, who make up the majority of Mù Cang Chải's population, have maintained a traditional way of life that is closely tied to the agricultural calendar and the rhythms of the surrounding landscape. Their villages, typically situated on the upper slopes above the terraced fields, consist of simple wooden and bamboo homes surrounded by small vegetable gardens and livestock enclosures. Traditional handicrafts — particularly the intricate embroidery and batik fabric work for which H'Mong women are renowned — remain an important part of both cultural identity and household income. Visitors to the area are often invited to observe or even participate in weaving and dyeing demonstrations, and locally produced textiles are among the most sought-after souvenirs available at village markets. Community-based tourism initiatives have increasingly sought to ensure that the economic benefits of tourism flow directly to local families rather than being captured entirely by outside operators.", "Accommodation options in Mù Cang Chải have expanded considerably in recent years to meet growing visitor demand. The district now offers a range of places to stay, from simple guesthouses run by local families to more comfortable eco-lodges with mountain views. Several homestay programs allow visitors to sleep, eat, and spend time with H'Mong families, providing an immersive cultural experience that is difficult to replicate in a conventional hotel setting. Many homestay hosts prepare traditional meals using locally grown ingredients — including dishes made with sticky rice, mountain herbs, and free-range poultry — and it is common practice for complimentary food such as seasonal fruit or herbal tea to be offered to guests upon arrival as a gesture of welcome and hospitality. This tradition of generous hosting is deeply embedded in H'Mong culture and is consistently mentioned by visitors as one of the most memorable aspects of their stay.", "Despite its growing popularity, Mù Cang Chải faces a number of challenges related to the management of tourism's impact on its fragile natural and cultural environment. The increased foot traffic on the terraced fields during peak season has caused measurable erosion in some areas, and local authorities have introduced designated walking paths and viewing platforms in an effort to reduce direct pressure on the most sensitive agricultural zones. There are also concerns that the rapid commercialization of local crafts and cultural performances risks turning authentic traditions into staged spectacles designed primarily for tourist consumption. Responsible travel organizations operating in the area emphasize the importance of supporting locally owned businesses, respecting community protocols around photography and private spaces, and visiting outside peak season when possible to distribute visitor numbers more evenly across the year.", "For many who visit, Mù Cang Chải leaves a lasting impression that goes beyond the visual splendor of its famous terraces. The combination of extraordinary natural beauty, a living agricultural tradition, and the warmth and generosity of its H'Mong communities creates an experience that feels genuinely rare in an era of increasingly standardized tourism. Whether arriving in the golden light of the harvest season or in the misty stillness of winter, visitors consistently find that this remote highland district offers something that cannot easily be found elsewhere — a sense of connection to a landscape and a way of life that have endured, and continue to evolve, across centuries."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To provide a balanced introduction to Mù Cang Chải, highlighting its natural beauty, cultural heritage, and the challenges it faces as a tourism destination.", "To persuade readers to visit Mù Cang Chải during the harvest season rather than at any other time of the year.", "To argue that Mù Cang Chải's growing popularity is causing irreversible damage to its natural environment and traditional culture.", "To compare Mù Cang Chải with other highland destinations in Southeast Asia and explain why it is the most visually spectacular of them."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the harvest season considered the most popular time to visit Mù Cang Chải?", options: ["The mountain roads are fully repaired during this period, making the journey to the district significantly easier and safer for visitors.", "Accommodation prices are substantially reduced during the harvest season as guesthouses compete to attract visitors from major cities.", "Local festivals and cultural performances organized specifically for tourists take place exclusively during the autumn harvest months.", "The ripening rice transforms the terraced fields into a vivid display of golden and amber colors that attracts photographers and nature lovers."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following traditional crafts are H'Mong women in Mù Cang Chải particularly well known for?", options: ["Silversmithing and jewelry making that produce ornamental items worn during festivals and other ceremonial occasions.", "Pottery and ceramic painting techniques that have been passed down through generations of H'Mong artisan families.", "Woodcarving and bamboo weaving used to construct and decorate the interior of traditional H'Mong village homes.", "Intricate embroidery and batik fabric work that forms an important part of both cultural identity and household income."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 3 (\"Their villages, typically situated on the upper slopes\") refers to _______.", options: ["the visitors who travel to the area and participate in homestay programs with local families", "the local authorities who have introduced regulations to manage the impact of tourism on agricultural land", "the H'Mong people, who make up the majority of Mù Cang Chải's permanent population", "the responsible travel organizations that operate community-based tourism initiatives in the district"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the passage say about food offered to homestay guests upon arrival?", options: ["Guests are expected to pay a small additional fee for food and drinks provided outside of the main meals included in their homestay package.", "Food offered to guests upon arrival is selected by village elders according to traditional ceremonial protocols observed during the harvest season.", "Items such as seasonal fruit or herbal tea are provided at no charge to guests as a customary expression of H'Mong hospitality.", "Arrival meals are prepared using imported ingredients to ensure that guests from outside the region find the flavors familiar and accessible."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what measure have local authorities introduced to address the problem of erosion on the terraced fields?", options: ["They have introduced a mandatory guide system requiring all visitors to be accompanied by a certified local guide when walking near the terraces.", "They have constructed protective barriers along the edges of the most fragile terrace sections to prevent accidental damage by tourists.", "They have imposed a strict annual limit on the total number of visitors permitted to enter the Mù Cang Chải district during the harvest season.", "They have established designated walking paths and viewing platforms to reduce direct foot traffic pressure on the most sensitive agricultural areas."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"sought-after\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["recently discovered by outside researchers who have begun documenting traditional H'Mong textile techniques for academic purposes", "heavily discounted in price because the high volume of production has made the items widely available throughout the region", "in great demand and highly desired by buyers who actively look for them as desirable and valued purchases", "difficult to find because local artisans produce only a small number of items each season for personal use rather than commercial sale"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"complimentary\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["specially selected to complement the flavors of the main dishes served during a traditional H'Mong family dinner", "provided to guests at no cost as part of a welcoming gesture or as an included feature of the accommodation", "offered in return for positive reviews or recommendations that guests are expected to share after their stay", "expressing admiration or praise for the quality of the food prepared by the host family during a traditional meal"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern or recommendation related to responsible tourism in Mù Cang Chải?", options: ["The importance of visiting outside peak season to help distribute visitor numbers more evenly throughout the year.", "The risk that rapid commercialization may transform authentic cultural practices into performances staged primarily for tourist entertainment.", "The value of respecting community guidelines around photography and access to private spaces within local villages.", "The need for a new government-funded visitor center to provide tourists with accurate information about local customs and regulations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's attitude toward Mù Cang Chải as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Cautiously pessimistic, suggesting that growing tourism pressure will inevitably erode the authenticity that currently makes the destination appealing.", "Genuinely admiring and appreciative, portraying Mù Cang Chải as a rare and meaningful destination while acknowledging the real challenges it faces.", "Strictly neutral and informational, presenting facts about the district without expressing any personal appreciation or concern.", "Primarily promotional, emphasizing the positive aspects of the district while deliberately avoiding discussion of any negative impacts of tourism."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Designing the Future City: Smart Technology, Sustainability, and the Human Factor", paragraphs: ["The idea of the \"future city\" has long occupied a central place in both popular imagination and serious urban planning discourse. From the utopian garden cities envisioned by Ebenezer Howard at the turn of the twentieth century to the gleaming, data-driven \"smart cities\" promoted by technology companies and municipal governments today, each era has projected onto the urban form its most urgent hopes and anxieties about how human beings can best organize collective life. What distinguishes the current moment from earlier waves of urban futurism, however, is the convergence of several genuinely transformative forces — digital connectivity, climate change, demographic pressure, and advances in materials science — that are reshaping the fundamental assumptions upon which cities have been planned and built for decades. The future city is no longer purely speculative; in significant and measurable ways, it is already under construction.", "At the center of contemporary urban futures discourse stands the concept of the smart city — a metropolitan environment in which physical infrastructure is integrated with digital sensors, data networks, and artificial intelligence systems to optimize the delivery of services, improve resource efficiency, and enhance the quality of daily life for residents. Smart traffic management systems that adjust signal timing in real time to reduce congestion, energy grids capable of dynamically balancing supply and demand across thousands of distributed generation points, and waste collection networks that deploy vehicles only when bins are full rather than on fixed schedules — these are among the more tangible applications already being piloted in cities from Singapore and Amsterdam to Songdo in South Korea and Masdar in the United Arab Emirates. Proponents argue that the data-driven optimization these systems enable can dramatically reduce a city's environmental footprint while simultaneously improving the efficiency and equity of service delivery across different neighborhoods.", "Yet the smart city model has attracted substantial criticism alongside its enthusiastic promotion. Privacy advocates have raised serious concerns about the extent to which sensor-saturated urban environments constitute, in effect, systems of continuous surveillance — generating granular data about residents' movements, behaviors, and daily routines that may be retained by governments or corporate platform operators for purposes extending well beyond service optimization. The experience of Sidewalk Toronto — an ambitious smart city development proposed by a subsidiary of Alphabet Inc. on Toronto's waterfront — is widely cited as an instructive case study: the project was ultimately abandoned in 2020 following sustained public opposition rooted substantially in concerns about data governance, corporate control of public infrastructure, and the absence of meaningful democratic oversight. This episode has prompted urban planners and policymakers in several countries to reconsider the terms on which private technology companies are permitted to participate in the design and management of public urban space.", "The sustainability imperative represents a separate but deeply intertwined strand of future urban thinking. Cities currently account for approximately seventy percent of global greenhouse gas emissions, making urban transformation an indispensable component of any credible strategy for addressing climate change. The concept of the fifteen-minute city — popularized by the Paris mayor Anne Hidalgo and urban scholar Carlos Moreno — offers one compelling vision of how cities might be reorganized around human scale and reduced carbon dependency: a model in which residents can access work, education, healthcare, retail, and recreation within a fifteen-minute walk or cycle from their homes, dramatically reducing the need for motorized transportation and its associated emissions. While the fifteen-minute city concept has been embraced by planners in Paris, Melbourne, and a number of other cities, critics have pointed out that its full implementation requires significant restructuring of land use, zoning regulations, and public investment patterns that may prove politically and economically difficult to achieve in practice.", "Green infrastructure — the deliberate integration of natural systems into the built urban environment — has emerged as another promising strategy for building cities that are both more sustainable and more livable. Urban forests, green roofs, constructed wetlands, and permeable paving systems can simultaneously reduce the urban heat island effect, manage stormwater, improve air quality, sequester carbon, and provide residents with access to natural spaces shown to deliver measurable benefits for mental health and wellbeing. Cities such as Singapore, Medellín in Colombia, and Melbourne in Australia have invested substantially in green infrastructure programs that have transformed previously neglected or degraded urban spaces into ecologically functional and publicly valued assets. The challenge lies in ensuring that these improvements are distributed equitably across different socioeconomic neighborhoods rather than being concentrated in already prosperous areas where property values stand to benefit most directly.", "Perhaps the most fundamental insight to emerge from serious engagement with the future city question is that no technology or design innovation, however sophisticated, can substitute for the quality of governance, democratic participation, and social trust that determines whether urban transformation serves the interests of all residents or primarily those of the most powerful. The cities most likely to navigate the challenges ahead successfully are not necessarily those with the most advanced sensors or the most ambitious architectural visions, but those in which residents have genuine agency over the decisions that shape their built environment, in which institutions are capable of distributing the benefits of urban investment fairly, and in which the diversity of human needs and aspirations is treated as a resource rather than a complication to be managed. The future city, in this sense, is less a technological destination than an ongoing social and political achievement.", "For urban planners, architects, and policymakers grappling with these challenges in real time, the temptation to reach for scalable technological solutions is understandable — and not entirely misguided. Digital tools, renewable energy systems, and smart infrastructure genuinely expand the range of what is physically possible in the built environment. But the evidence from cities that have pursued technological modernization without adequate attention to equity, participation, and governance suggests that the gap between a city that is merely efficient and one that is genuinely good to live in is bridged not by algorithms alone, but by the sustained, difficult, and irreplaceable work of democratic urban politics."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Smart city technology represents the most effective and comprehensive solution available for addressing the environmental and social challenges facing modern cities.", "Cities in Europe and Asia are already fully prepared for the demands of the future, having invested heavily in both green infrastructure and digital connectivity over the past decade.", "The primary obstacle to building better cities is not a lack of technology or design innovation but rather the resistance of established real estate and energy industries to meaningful urban reform.", "The future city is being shaped by converging forces including digital technology and climate change, but its success ultimately depends on governance, equity, and democratic participation as much as on innovation."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is given as an example of a smart city application already being tested in real urban environments?", options: ["Underground freight delivery systems that remove commercial vehicles from street level and reduce congestion in city center areas.", "Waste collection networks that dispatch vehicles based on real-time data about bin capacity rather than on fixed predetermined schedules.", "Automated housing allocation systems that match residents to available properties based on their commuting patterns and lifestyle preferences.", "Public health monitoring platforms that detect early signs of disease outbreaks by analyzing anonymized movement data from residents' mobile devices."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the broader pattern of corporate involvement in public urban infrastructure that has characterized smart city development in multiple countries", "the abandonment of the Sidewalk Toronto project following sustained public opposition centered on data privacy and corporate control concerns", "the decision by privacy advocates to formally challenge the data governance practices of major technology companies operating in urban environments", "the publication of new regulatory guidelines by the Canadian government restricting the use of private sensor networks in public spaces"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the central principle underlying the fifteen-minute city concept?", options: ["All essential urban services should be delivered digitally within fifteen minutes of a resident submitting a request through a municipal smartphone application.", "Residents should be able to reach work, education, healthcare, and other daily necessities within a fifteen-minute walk or cycle from their homes.", "Public transportation networks should be redesigned to connect all major urban services within a maximum journey time of fifteen minutes.", "Urban neighborhoods should be designed so that residents spend no more than fifteen minutes per day traveling between different areas of the city."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following benefits of green infrastructure is explicitly mentioned in the passage?", options: ["Generating renewable energy from solar panels and wind turbines integrated into green roof installations across the city.", "Reducing the financial cost of maintaining conventional drainage and stormwater management systems across urban neighborhoods.", "Attracting high-income residents and international investment to previously neglected neighborhoods by increasing local property values.", "Improving air quality, managing stormwater, and providing natural spaces that deliver measurable mental health benefits for residents."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why was the Sidewalk Toronto project ultimately abandoned?", options: ["The project was cancelled due to escalating construction costs that made the development financially unviable for the private investors involved.", "Public opposition focused on concerns about data governance, corporate control of public infrastructure, and insufficient democratic oversight led to the project's cancellation.", "Disagreements between the municipal government and Alphabet Inc. over the ownership and monetization of commercially generated urban data made agreement impossible.", "The project was blocked by federal government regulators who determined that the proposed sensor network violated existing national privacy legislation."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of integrating green infrastructure into urban environments?", options: ["Generating additional public revenue through entrance fees charged to residents who wish to access newly created urban green spaces.", "Providing residents with access to natural environments that have been shown to support mental health and personal wellbeing.", "Sequestering carbon and contributing to a city's broader strategy for reducing its overall greenhouse gas emissions.", "Reducing the urban heat island effect that causes city temperatures to rise significantly above those of surrounding rural areas."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"tangible\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["concrete and real, existing in a form that can be directly observed, measured, or experienced in the physical world", "sufficiently advanced in design to be considered genuinely innovative compared with the urban planning practices of previous decades", "commercially profitable enough to attract sustained private sector investment without the need for government subsidies", "theoretically promising but not yet supported by sufficient empirical evidence to justify large-scale investment and deployment"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"indispensable\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["broadly supported across the political spectrum and therefore likely to attract cross-party legislative endorsement in most democracies", "absolutely essential and impossible to do without when attempting to achieve a particular goal or outcome", "technically demanding and therefore requiring specialized expertise and substantial financial investment to implement successfully", "politically controversial and likely to face significant resistance from established industries and vested urban stakeholders"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of technology's role in building future cities?", options: ["The author values technological tools as genuinely useful but argues that they are insufficient on their own and must be accompanied by strong democratic governance and a commitment to social equity.", "The author suggests that the future city debate has been dominated by technology companies whose commercial interests have distorted public policy away from more effective non-digital approaches.", "The author considers technological innovation to be the single most important driver of urban improvement and argues that governance concerns should not slow its adoption.", "The author believes that digital and smart technologies are fundamentally incompatible with the goal of creating equitable and democratically governed cities."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "More Than Music: The Communicative Functions of Birdsong", paragraphs: ["To the untrained ear, the dawn chorus that fills woodlands and gardens each spring morning might seem like little more than an exuberant outpouring of natural sound — pleasant, perhaps even uplifting, but essentially decorative. Scientists who have devoted their careers to studying avian communication have reached a very different conclusion. Birdsong, as research over the past several decades has made increasingly clear, is a richly functional communication system serving a wide range of biological purposes that extend well beyond the single goal of attracting a reproductive partner. Individual vocalizations carry precise information about territorial boundaries, the identity of the singer, the presence and nature of potential threats, and the coordination of group movement — each decoded by other birds with a speed and accuracy that continues to astonish researchers. [1] Understanding the full breadth of these communicative functions has reshaped how biologists think about the cognitive complexity of non-human animals.", "Perhaps the most extensively documented function of birdsong is territorial advertisement. In the majority of songbird species studied, males sing persistently from prominent perches at the edges of their territory during the breeding season, broadcasting a continuous acoustic declaration of occupancy to potential rivals. The effectiveness of this strategy has been demonstrated experimentally: when researchers remove a resident male from his territory and replace his presence with a speaker broadcasting his song, neighboring males are significantly less likely to encroach on the vacated space than when the territory is left acoustically silent. The potency of song as a territorial signal derives in part from the information it conveys about the singer's physical condition and fighting ability — attributes that rival males assess and weigh before deciding whether to challenge or retreat. In species with larger song repertoires, the complexity of the vocal display itself appears to function as an honest signal of competitive quality, discouraging challenges from rivals who recognize themselves as likely to be outmatched.", "Beyond territorial defense, birds employ a sophisticated repertoire of alarm vocalizations that serve to warn conspecifics — members of the same species — as well as members of other species about the presence of predators. Ornithologists have demonstrated that many songbirds produce acoustically distinct alarm calls depending on whether the threat is aerial, such as a hawk or falcon, or terrestrial, such as a cat or a snake. Aerial predator calls tend to be high-pitched, thin, and difficult to locate — acoustic properties that make the caller hard to pinpoint while the warning is simultaneously broadcast to nearby individuals. Ground predator calls, by contrast, are typically lower in frequency, harsher in quality, and easier to localize, enabling other birds to orient themselves rapidly toward the source of the threat. These functional distinctions in alarm call structure have even been found to be understood across species lines: chickadees responding to the alarm calls of titmice, for instance, or European great tits attending to the warning signals of blackbirds sharing the same woodland habitat. [2]", "Contact calls represent a further category of avian vocalization that serves fundamentally different communicative purposes from song. Typically shorter, quieter, and structurally simpler than full territorial song, contact calls are used primarily to maintain cohesion within flocks — allowing individual birds to signal their position and keep track of companions as they forage through vegetation where visual contact is frequently lost. In migratory species, contact calls appear to play a particularly vital role during nocturnal migration, enabling birds flying in darkness to orient themselves relative to others of their species and avoid becoming isolated from the group. Studies of flight calls recorded during peak migration nights have revealed that these vocalizations can encode species identity, individual identity, and even directional information in ways that allow listeners to track the composition and heading of passing flocks with remarkable precision. [3]", "The phenomenon of duetting — in which paired birds, most commonly in tropical species, produce precisely coordinated vocal exchanges that interlock to form a unified acoustic structure — introduces yet another functional dimension to the study of avian communication. Duets are produced with a temporal precision that requires each participant to anticipate the other's contributions and adjust their own timing accordingly, implying a degree of social coordination and mutual attentiveness that was once considered uniquely human. Researchers have proposed several non-mutually exclusive explanations for the function of duetting: it may reinforce the pair bond between partners, coordinate reproductive activities, serve as a joint territorial signal that is more difficult for rivals to counterfeit than an individual's solo song, or allow each partner to monitor the other's location and social fidelity continuously. The relative weight of each of these functions appears to vary considerably across species and ecological contexts. [4]", "The study of birdsong functions has also yielded important insights into the question of cultural transmission in non-human animals. Because many songbird species learn their vocalizations from adult tutors rather than inheriting them genetically, the specific calls and songs used within a population to signal territory, alarm, or group identity can vary geographically in ways that reflect local learning history rather than genetic difference. This has led some researchers to argue that birdsong represents a genuine form of non-human culture — a body of socially transmitted information that shapes behavior and communication in ways structurally analogous, if not identical, to the role of culture in human societies. The implications of this perspective extend beyond ornithology, raising fundamental questions about the cognitive prerequisites for culture and the degree to which the capacity for flexible, learned communication is a distinctively human achievement or a more broadly distributed feature of animal cognition.", "What emerges from this body of research is a picture of avian vocal communication as a system of considerable sophistication — one that is simultaneously shaped by evolutionary pressures acting over millions of years and by individual learning and social experience accumulated within a single lifetime. The diversity of functions that birdsong serves, from territorial advertisement and predator warning to flock coordination and pair-bond maintenance, reflects the breadth and complexity of the social and ecological challenges that birds must navigate in their daily lives. Far from being mere background music, the sounds that fill natural habitats represent an ongoing, dynamic conversation — one that science is only beginning to decode in its full richness and depth."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Recent advances in recording technology have allowed scientists to finally decode the precise informational content of birdsong after decades of inconclusive research.", "Birdsong is primarily a territorial behavior that has been secondarily adapted over evolutionary time to serve a limited number of additional communicative purposes.", "Birdsong constitutes a sophisticated and multifunctional communication system serving a diverse range of biological purposes beyond mate attraction alone.", "The cognitive complexity revealed by birdsong research demonstrates that the communicative abilities of birds are equivalent in all meaningful respects to those of human beings."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the experiment involving a speaker broadcasting a removed male's song demonstrate?", options: ["Neighboring males are significantly less likely to enter a vacated territory when the resident's song continues to be broadcast than when the territory is left silent.", "Rival males are capable of distinguishing between a live singer and a recorded playback and respond more aggressively to recordings than to live competitors.", "The removal of a resident male triggers an immediate escalation in singing activity among all neighboring males competing for the newly available territory.", "The acoustic quality of a song broadcast through a speaker is sufficiently degraded that neighboring males no longer recognize it as a reliable territorial signal."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do aerial predator alarm calls differ acoustically from ground predator alarm calls?", options: ["Aerial predator calls are produced at much higher volume and repeated more rapidly than ground predator calls in order to mobilize a larger group response.", "Aerial predator calls are structurally simpler and shorter in duration, while ground predator calls involve more complex sequences of notes and phrases.", "Aerial predator calls are high-pitched and difficult to localize, whereas ground predator calls are lower in frequency and easier to locate directionally.", "Aerial predator calls are understood only by members of the same species, whereas ground predator calls are recognized across multiple co-inhabiting species."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the various species of aerial and terrestrial predators that regularly threaten songbird populations in woodland habitats", "the ornithologists who have conducted experimental studies comparing alarm call responses across different bird species", "the functional distinctions in acoustic structure that differentiate alarm calls produced in response to aerial versus ground-based threats", "the chickadees, titmice, great tits, and blackbirds cited as examples of species that share the same woodland environment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role do contact calls play for migratory birds during nocturnal migration?", options: ["They allow birds to locate food sources in unfamiliar territories by broadcasting signals that are answered by resident species familiar with local conditions.", "They allow birds to communicate their intended destination to other members of the flock, ensuring that the group reaches the same wintering site each year.", "They enable migrating birds to maintain orientation relative to others of their species and avoid becoming separated from the group while flying in darkness.", "They serve as navigation aids by encoding information about wind direction and atmospheric pressure that helps birds adjust their flight path accordingly."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what characteristic of duetting implies a significant degree of social coordination between the two participating birds?", options: ["The temporal precision required for each bird to anticipate and interlock with the other's contributions, suggesting active mutual attentiveness.", "The fact that duets are produced exclusively during the breeding season, suggesting they are directly linked to reproductive hormonal cycles.", "The geographical consistency of duet structure within a species, indicating that the songs are genetically encoded rather than learned from social partners.", "The finding that duets are produced at significantly greater volume than solo songs, implying a cooperative amplification of the territorial signal."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"potency\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the acoustic range over which a territorial song can be detected by rival males under typical woodland habitat conditions", "the frequency with which a resident male repeats his territorial song throughout the duration of a single breeding day", "the degree of complexity and structural elaboration present in a male's vocal display during peak territorial competition", "the effectiveness or power of a signal in producing a strong and reliable response in those who receive it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"counterfeit\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to falsely imitate or replicate something in order to deceive others into accepting it as the genuine article", "to systematically analyze and decode the informational content of a complex acoustic signal produced by another individual", "to gradually replace an established behavioral pattern with a newer and more advantageous alternative over successive generations", "to physically interrupt or disrupt by interjecting competing sounds into an ongoing vocal exchange between paired partners"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a proposed function of duetting behavior in birds?", options: ["Serving as a joint territorial signal that rivals find more difficult to imitate than a solo performance.", "Attracting additional unpaired individuals to the territory to increase the genetic diversity of the local population.", "Allowing each partner to continuously monitor the other's location and assess their commitment to the pair bond.", "Reinforcing the social bond between mated partners through regular coordinated vocal interaction."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This cross-species responsiveness suggests that the acoustic properties of certain alarm calls may have evolved under shared selective pressure from common predators rather than independently in each lineage.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Uncorking the Past: The Authentication of Ancient Wine and What It Reveals About Social Hierarchy", paragraphs: ["Among the many artifacts that archaeologists excavate from ancient sites, fermented beverages — and the vessels, residues, and written records associated with them — have proven to be among the most informationally dense. Wine, beer, and other fermented drinks were not merely dietary staples in many ancient civilizations; they were laden with social, religious, and political meaning, their production, distribution, and consumption governed by elaborate protocols that encoded and reproduced the hierarchies of the societies that made them. The authentication of ancient wine — establishing that a particular residue or vessel genuinely contains or once contained fermented grape product — therefore opens a window not only onto ancient technology and trade but onto the social architecture of antiquity itself. What researchers have discovered in attempting to answer the deceptively simple question of how old a wine truly is has, in several celebrated cases, illuminated the strikingly asymmetric ways in which access to this prestige commodity was distributed along lines of gender and social rank. [1]", "The methods by which scientists establish the antiquity and composition of ancient wine residues have grown considerably more sophisticated over the past three decades. Chemical analysis using infrared spectroscopy and liquid chromatography can identify the molecular signatures of tartaric acid — a compound present in high concentrations in grapes but rare in other organic materials — as well as associated organic acids and phenolic compounds that together constitute a chemical fingerprint for wine. Mass spectrometry enables researchers to analyze stable isotope ratios within residue samples, providing information about the geographic origin of the grapes from which the wine was made and, in some cases, about the climate and soil conditions of the production region. Radiocarbon dating of organic residues, when combined with stratigraphic analysis of the surrounding archaeological context, allows the age of a deposit to be established with a precision that earlier generations of archaeologists could not have imagined. [2] Together, these methodologies allow researchers to move from the mere identification of a stain on pottery to a nuanced reconstruction of an ancient beverage's origin, age, and composition.", "One of the most extensively discussed case studies in ancient wine authentication involves residues recovered from elite burial contexts in the ancient Near East and Egypt. At the tomb of the Scorpion I pharaoh at Abydos, dated to approximately 3150 BCE, archaeologists discovered hundreds of imported Canaanite amphorae containing residues that chemical analysis subsequently confirmed to be wine — making this one of the earliest securely dated evidence of wine consumption in Egypt and demonstrating that access to imported wine was, from the earliest periods of Egyptian statehood, a marker of the very highest social position. The quantity of wine present — estimated at over four thousand liters — and the evidence of its importation from the Levant across a considerable geographic distance underscored the degree to which control over long-distance trade networks was itself a dimension of royal power. [3] Crucially, the spatial patterning of the wine jars within the tomb, analyzed alongside other grave goods, provided evidence that this extraordinary cache of prestige goods was assembled exclusively for the burial of a single male individual of the highest social rank.", "The gendered dimension of ancient wine consumption has attracted particular scholarly attention in recent years, partly because the evidence recovered from archaeological contexts consistently complicates the idealized symmetry sometimes projected onto ancient societies by modern interpreters. In many ancient Mediterranean and Near Eastern cultures, textual and iconographic sources suggest that wine drinking was normatively associated with male sociability — symposia in classical Greece, for instance, were explicitly male-dominated drinking occasions from which respectable women were largely excluded, even as female slaves and entertainers might be present in a servile capacity. Yet the archaeological evidence occasionally diverges from this normative picture in instructive ways. Bioarchaeological analysis of dental calculus — the mineralized deposits that form on teeth and preserve traces of consumed foods and beverages — has revealed in several cases that female individuals buried in apparently high-status contexts had consumed wine regularly during their lifetimes, despite belonging to cultures whose textual record suggests that respectable women were expected to abstain. [4]", "A particularly striking example emerged from the analysis of a late Iron Age burial at Vix in Burgundy, France, dating to approximately 480 BCE. The burial, which contained one of the largest ancient Greek bronze kraters ever found — a vessel used for mixing wine with water at elite drinking events — was long assumed by early excavators to belong to a male warrior or chieftain on the grounds that such an exceptional concentration of prestige goods would be consistent only with male high-status burial. Subsequent skeletal analysis established conclusively that the buried individual was female. This reattribution prompted a fundamental reassessment of assumptions about the relationship between gender and access to wine-associated prestige in Iron Age Europe, suggesting that at least some elite women in this period participated actively in the wine-centered feasting culture that underpinned political alliance and social identity among the regional aristocracy. Whether the Vix burial represents an isolated anomaly or evidence of a broader pattern of female participation in elite wine culture that the textual record has systematically obscured remains a subject of ongoing scholarly debate.", "The methodological convergence of chemical residue analysis, bioarchaeology, isotope geochemistry, and careful contextual reading of burial assemblages has transformed ancient wine studies from a largely anecdotal enterprise into a rigorous interdisciplinary field. What was once established — or fabricated — through provenance documents of dubious authenticity can now be interrogated through multiple independent lines of physical evidence that are considerably harder to falsify. The implications extend beyond the academy: the authentication of ancient wine has become a significant concern in the commercial art and antiquities market, where the value of a vessel may be dramatically inflated by claims of exceptional antiquity or royal association that chemical and isotopic analysis is increasingly able to confirm or refute . In both scholarly and commercial contexts, the question of how one proves that something is genuinely ancient has acquired an urgency and a methodological sophistication that would have been unimaginable to the first archaeologists who lifted these vessels from the earth.", "More broadly, the study of ancient wine as a social artifact reminds us that the material culture of consumption is never merely material. The vessel, the residue, the grave in which both were deposited, and the body interred alongside them together constitute a document — imperfect, fragmentary, and requiring careful interpretation — of the social world that produced them. Reading that document with the full range of analytical tools now available offers one of the most direct routes yet discovered into the lived experience of hierarchies that written history has frequently simplified, distorted, or rendered invisible."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The authentication of ancient wine is primarily valuable as a commercial tool for establishing the market value of rare archaeological artifacts in the global antiquities trade.", "Ancient wine studies has been transformed from a speculative discipline into a rigorous science primarily through the development of radiocarbon dating techniques that can establish age with unprecedented precision.", "Scientific advances in wine residue analysis have demonstrated conclusively that women in most ancient societies consumed wine as frequently as men, contradicting centuries of historical assumption.", "The scientific authentication of ancient wine, combined with careful contextual analysis, reveals not only the composition and age of ancient beverages but the social hierarchies — including gendered ones — that governed their consumption."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is tartaric acid considered a particularly significant marker in the chemical analysis of ancient residues?", options: ["It reacts distinctively with the clay compounds in ancient pottery, producing a measurable chemical signature that can be identified through isotope analysis.", "It is present in high concentrations in grapes but rare in other organic materials, making it a reliable chemical indicator of wine rather than other substances.", "It is produced exclusively during the fermentation process and therefore indicates that a liquid has been deliberately processed rather than simply stored.", "It remains chemically stable over extremely long periods of time, allowing it to be detected accurately in residues that are many thousands of years old."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the discovery at the tomb of Scorpion I at Abydos reveal about the relationship between wine and social status in early Egypt?", options: ["It demonstrates that wine production was already well established within Egypt by 3150 BCE and did not depend on imports from neighboring regions.", "It provides evidence that wine was consumed communally across different social classes in early Egyptian society rather than being restricted to the elite.", "It suggests that the Egyptian royal court maintained direct diplomatic relationships with Canaanite wine producers through a system of formalized trade agreements.", "It shows that access to imported wine was, from the earliest periods of Egyptian statehood, a marker of the very highest social and political position."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"laden\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["formally designated by institutional authorities as carrying a specific and legally recognized symbolic function", "carefully concealed within or beneath a surface layer of more ordinary and socially neutral material culture", "gradually accumulated over long periods of time through the repeated association of objects with prestigious social occasions", "heavily loaded or filled with a significant amount of a particular quality, meaning, or associative content"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has bioarchaeological analysis of dental calculus revealed about female consumption of wine in some ancient cultures?", options: ["Elite women in ancient Greece regularly consumed wine at symposia but were required to do so in separate rooms away from the male participants.", "Women in ancient Mediterranean cultures developed a distinct tradition of wine consumption in exclusively female ritual contexts that left no trace in the textual record.", "Female servants and entertainers at ancient drinking events consumed greater quantities of wine than the male guests they were employed to serve.", "Women buried in high-status contexts in several cultures show evidence of regular wine consumption despite textual sources suggesting respectable women were expected to abstain."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did early excavators initially assume the Vix burial belonged to a male individual?", options: ["The burial contained weapons and armor that were conventionally associated with male warrior burials in Iron Age European archaeological tradition.", "Written inscriptions found within the burial explicitly identified the occupant using masculine grammatical forms in the regional Iron Age script.", "The exceptional concentration of prestige goods, including a large Greek wine-mixing vessel, was considered by excavators to be consistent only with male high-status burial.", "The skeletal remains were too fragmentary at the time of excavation to permit a reliable biological sex determination by the methods then available."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the scholarly significance of establishing that the Vix burial belonged to a female individual?", options: ["It provided the first definitive archaeological evidence that women in Iron Age Europe were capable of independently accumulating wealth through long-distance trade.", "It prompted a reassessment of assumptions about the relationship between gender and access to wine-associated prestige in Iron Age Europe.", "It confirmed the hypothesis that Iron Age European societies were organized along matrilineal kinship principles that gave elite women control over feasting resources.", "It demonstrated that Greek bronze vessels were exported to female as well as male consumers, fundamentally revising our understanding of ancient Mediterranean trade networks."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"refute\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to reinterpret existing evidence in a way that assigns it a different meaning without directly challenging the accuracy of the original findings", "to formally withdraw a previously published claim from the academic record following the discovery of procedural errors in the original study", "to subject a hypothesis to a series of rigorous experimental tests in order to determine whether it is supported by empirical data", "to prove that a claim or assertion is false or incorrect by presenting contradictory evidence that undermines its validity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a scientific method used in the authentication and analysis of ancient wine residues?", options: ["Radiocarbon dating of organic residues combined with stratigraphic analysis to establish the age of a deposit.", "DNA sequencing of preserved grape seeds to determine the specific cultivar varieties used in ancient wine production.", "Mass spectrometry used to analyze stable isotope ratios and reconstruct the geographic origin of the grapes used to produce the wine.", "Infrared spectroscopy and liquid chromatography used to identify the molecular signatures of compounds associated with wine."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This spatial and contextual evidence thus provides a striking illustration of how the control of luxury imports functioned as a constitutive element of early royal authority rather than merely as its outward expression.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
